---
import AppDocLayout from '../../layouts/AppDocLayout.astro';
---

<AppDocLayout
  title="Stretch Easy - Support"
  description="Get help with Stretch Easy - Guided Stretching Routines iOS app."
  ogUrl="https://idlefusion.com/stretch-easy/support"
>
  <main>
    <section class="section-padding">
      <div class="max-w-2xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
        <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-[var(--text-primary)] mb-8">Support</h1>

        <div class="text-[var(--text-secondary)] leading-relaxed mb-12">
          <p class="text-lg mb-4">Need help with Stretch Easy?</p>
          <p>Send us an email at <a href="mailto:support@idlefusion.com?subject=Stretch%20Easy%20Support%20Request" class="text-[var(--accent-primary)] hover:underline">support@idlefusion.com</a> and we'll get back to you as soon as we can.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--text-primary)] mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div>
              <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)] mb-2">What routines are included?</h3>
              <p class="text-[var(--text-secondary)]">Five: Morning Wake-Up (5 minutes), Desk Break (3 minutes), Post-Workout (10 minutes), Full Body (16 minutes) and Evening Wind-Down (8 minutes). Today suggests one for the time of day: Morning Wake-Up before 11, Desk Break through the day, and Evening Wind-Down from 5 PM.</p>
            </div>

            <div>
              <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)] mb-2">Can I make my own routines?</h3>
              <p class="text-[var(--text-secondary)]">Yes. Tap the compose button at the top of Today, name the routine, add stretches from the library of 16, and set each one's time and the rest between them. To change or delete one of your routines, open it and use the More button. You can also open Target areas, pick the areas that feel tight, and start a routine made for them.</p>
            </div>

            <div>
              <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)] mb-2">How do the timers work?</h3>
              <p class="text-[var(--text-secondary)]">Each stretch counts down in a ring, with how to do it underneath. When time is up you get a haptic and a sound, a short rest, then the next stretch. The last three seconds tick. Pause, or Skip to move on. The screen stays on until the routine ends. In Settings you can turn off sounds, haptics, countdown sounds, or auto-advance, which makes each stretch wait for you to tap Next.</p>
            </div>

            <div>
              <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)] mb-2">How does the streak work?</h3>
              <p class="text-[var(--text-secondary)]">Your streak counts the days in a row you finished at least one routine. Today shows it under the headline: a filled leaf once you've stretched today, an outlined one while today's stretch is still to do. History shows your personal best and sixteen weeks of stretched days. A routine you end early doesn't count.</p>
            </div>

            <div>
              <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)] mb-2">What does Apple Health get?</h3>
              <p class="text-[var(--text-secondary)]">Nothing, unless you turn it on. In Settings, under Apple Health, turn on Save to Apple Health. The first time, iOS asks for permission to save workouts. After that, each routine you finish is saved to Apple Health as a Flexibility workout with its start time and length. Stretch Easy never reads your health data.</p>
            </div>

            <div>
              <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)] mb-2">I said no to Apple Health. How do I turn it on?</h3>
              <p class="text-[var(--text-secondary)]">Open the Health app, tap your picture at the top, then Apps, then Stretch Easy, and turn on Workouts. Then turn on Save to Apple Health in Stretch Easy's Settings. If you tap the switch while access is off, Stretch Easy shows these steps with a button that opens the Health app.</p>
            </div>

            <div>
              <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)] mb-2">Does the Apple Watch app work on its own?</h3>
              <p class="text-[var(--text-secondary)]">Yes. Open Stretch Easy on your Apple Watch, pick one of the five routines, and follow along with a tap on your wrist for each new stretch, even with your wrist down. Routines you finish on the watch stay on the watch for now; they don't show up in your iPhone's history.</p>
            </div>

            <div>
              <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)] mb-2">Can I change the look?</h3>
              <p class="text-[var(--text-secondary)]">Yes. In Settings, choose System, Light or Dark, and a Graphite or Black dark style. Accent offers eight colors, and each one comes with its own app icon.</p>
            </div>

            <div>
              <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)] mb-2">Is Stretch Easy medical advice?</h3>
              <p class="text-[var(--text-secondary)]">No. Stretch gently and stop if anything hurts. For pain that lasts, see a doctor or physical therapist.</p>
            </div>

            <div>
              <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)] mb-2">Is there a subscription?</h3>
              <p class="text-[var(--text-secondary)]">No. Stretch Easy is free, with no ads, no subscriptions and no in-app purchases.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</AppDocLayout>
